The per-scenario view shows a pattern the single-scenario version could not: \textbf{the ranking of architectures depends on which modality carries the signal}. The centralized baseline is best on \texttt{fight\_01}, \texttt{abandoned\_object\_01}, \texttt{intrusion\_01}, and \texttt{combined\_audio\_video\_01}---the video-dominant scenarios. All three MAS variants beat it on \texttt{audio\_glass\_break\_01} (0.800 vs.\ 0.000) and \texttt{audio\_alarm\_siren\_01}. Averaging across such a corpus produces a number whose value is largely determined by the corpus's modality mix, which is a property of the experiment design rather than of the architectures.

