\section{Conclusion}

Evaluated across nine scenarios with 373 runs and a paired inferential analysis, the results are as follows. The hierarchical \gls{mas} is \emph{not} demonstrably faster to alert than a centralized pipeline once more than one scenario is considered (RQ1: unresolved, confounded by pacing). Auction-based coordination is \emph{not} shown to improve precision or false-alert rate, and its central mechanism was inactive during measurement, making the claim untestable as implemented (RQ2: retracted, unresolved). Cross-modal fusion works but its contribution to confidence is unmeasurable because the noisy-OR saturates, and cross-modal alerts are rare (RQ3: mechanism confirmed, effect not measurable). The explanation guardrail is structurally sound and fired correctly on the single occasion a real model was tried, but was never exercised during the campaign (RQ4: design supported, empirically unevaluated). The privacy and audit pipeline held across all 373 runs, subject to efficacy being asserted rather than measured (RQ5: supported).

Three of five research questions therefore return negative or unresolved verdicts. The apparatus that produced those verdicts---and that made them visible where a single favourable run had concealed them---is the result this chapter is most confident in. The final chapter revises the contribution claims accordingly and sets out what each unresolved question would require.
